# Copy to .env next to compose.yaml. Nothing here is a secret of ours: every value is yours to choose.

# Signs session cookies. Any long random string: openssl rand -hex 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# ── The model: pick ONE block ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# A. Local, keyless, on CPU (docker compose --profile local up). llama.cpp downloads the GGUF named in
#    compose.yaml (LOCAL_MODEL_REPO / LOCAL_MODEL_FILE / LOCAL_MODEL_ALIAS override it).
OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://llama:8080/v1
OPENAI_API_KEY=
MODEL_ALLOWLIST=qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct

# B. Any hosted OpenAI-compatible provider (docker compose up). Mistral's La Plateforme, for example:
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.mistral.ai/v1
# OPENAI_API_KEY=<your Mistral key>
# MODEL_ALLOWLIST=mistral-large-2512,mistral-small-2506
#    or Hugging Face's router, with your own HF token (an id GET /models lists):
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://router.huggingface.co/v1
# OPENAI_API_KEY=<your Hugging Face token>
# MODEL_ALLOWLIST=Qwen/Qwen3-14B

# C. Coming from Open WebUI? Keep its names instead of A or B and leave OPENAI_BASE_URL unset. Lists are
#    ';'-separated with one key per URL (an empty key is a keyless endpoint). Chat offers the models of
#    every URL, and an id two of them list goes to the earlier one. When OPENAI_BASE_URL is
#    also set, it wins. Fewer keys than URLs leaves the rest keyless, and extra keys are dropped, as Open WebUI does.
# OPENAI_API_BASE_URLS=https://api.mistral.ai/v1
# OPENAI_API_KEYS=<your Mistral key>
# MODEL_ALLOWLIST=*
#    With Auto on, a turn Auto grades as a large task can go to one of those models, such as a frontier
#    model on a second URL. A chat that sent one stays on that model for its follow-ups, which keeps its
#    prompt cache; other chats stay on the default model:
# AUTO_LARGE_TASK_MODEL=<a model id one of the URLs lists>

# ── Uploads and My files: your scan decision ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A self-host takes file uploads (chat attachments, My files) once you decide how they are checked for
# child sexual abuse material; until then uploads stay off. This file decides for you so the stack works
# on first start: CSAM_SCAN=off stores uploads with NO hash check, each picture logs a line saying so, and
# you own detecting and reporting CSAM. Read the line before you open the stack to anyone else. Either:
CSAM_SCAN=off
#    or remove that line and name a hash-matching scanner (not part of this directory). Once named, it
#    checks every picture and refuses the upload when it cannot answer:
# CSAM_SCAN_PROVIDER=hma
# CSAM_SCAN_BASE_URL=
# CSAM_SCAN_TOKEN=
#    or turn uploads off altogether:
# MULTIMODAL_ENABLED=false

# ── Adult content ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# With a Shieldstral endpoint (GUARD_SHADOW_URL), chat declines sexually explicit text and nude or
# sexually posed pictures, and refuses a picture those checks could not answer for. To allow adult
# content on your instance, uncomment this. Child-safety screening stays on either way.
# ADULT_CONTENT_SCREEN=off

# The database is the bundled Postgres 17 in the `db` volume. To use a Postgres you already run, name it
# and turn the bundled one off; the app creates its tables there on first start. backup.sh and
# restore.sh then skip the database and still handle the file volumes: back it up on its own server.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://alpha:<password>@db.example.org:5432/alpha
# BUNDLED_DB=0

# Files are kept in the `files` volume (CUSTODIAL_STORAGE_BACKEND=local, compose's default). To keep them
# in an S3-compatible bucket, use block S with `docker compose --profile s3 ...` for the bundled
# SeaweedFS, or point CUSTODIAL_S3_ENDPOINT at your own server (leave it unset for AWS S3).
# S. An S3-compatible bucket. The key pair is SeaweedFS's admin identity and the app's credential; make
#    the secret a long random string (openssl rand -hex 32). With either left blank, --profile s3's
#    SeaweedFS refuses to start. FILES_ACCESS_KEY and FILES_SECRET_KEY, the names deploy/.env uses, are
#    accepted in place of the AWS_* pair.
# CUSTODIAL_STORAGE_BACKEND=s3
# CUSTODIAL_S3_BUCKET=alpha-files
# CUSTODIAL_S3_ENDPOINT=http://seaweedfs:8333
# CUSTODIAL_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=alpha
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# deploy/'s names for the same pair, read when the two above are unset (an .env carried over from it):
# FILES_ACCESS_KEY=
# FILES_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PORT=3000
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=alpha   # read only when the db volume is first created: set it before the first start,
#                           # and change it later with ./db-passwd.sh (README.md, Configuration)
# DEPLOY_ENV=production   # a production boot refuses without ALPHA_EDITION, so set it too:
# ALPHA_EDITION=selfhost  # "hosted" is only for Current AI's own deployments. With selfhost and no safety
#                         # classifier (below), turns go unscreened, and boot logs SELF-HOST, NO SCREEN
# STATUS_PAGE_URL=https://status.example.org   # your Better Stack page for the incident banner. Default: none
# LOCAL_MODELS_DIR=/path/to/gguf-cache   # a host directory for the GGUF in place of the `models` volume
# BRAND_ORGANISATION_NAME=Example Org   # who runs this install, shown in chat and told to the model. Default: Current AI
# BRAND_PRODUCT_NAME=Example Chat       # the app's name in the header, sidebar, page title and sign-in email. Default: AI Potluck
#                                       # (an Open WebUI WEBUI_NAME is read as this when it is unset)
# BRAND_ASSISTANT_NAME=Ada              # a name the assistant answers to; it still names its model. Default: none

# ── Features (selfhost/README.md explains each and lists every flag's default) ───────────────────
# With no PostHog every flag reads its self-host default; ALPHA_FLAGS overrides single flags
# (alphaFlagOverrides in web/src/lib/chat/server/flags.ts). "+key" on, "-key" off, comma-separated.
# ALPHA_FLAGS=-document-attachments

# Uploads follow the scan decision above (ALPHA_EDITION=selfhost turns MULTIMODAL_ENABLED on unless you
# set it; multimodalMode.ts). PDF and DOCX are read to text and sent with the turn; document-attachments
# is on in self-host, and ALPHA_FLAGS=-document-attachments turns it off (documentAttachmentsGate.ts).

# The safety classifier: set one and every turn is screened, no flag needed (guardGate.ts). Shieldstral
# screens text and pictures; S1 screens text only, turns s1-safety-judge on when both of its lines are
# set, and then text goes to S1 alone. With neither, a self-host answers unscreened.
# With --profile safety, the bundled Shieldstral on CPU (README.md, "A safety screen"):
# GUARD_SHADOW_URL=http://shieldstral:8080
# ZENTROPI_S1_ENDPOINT_URL=
# HF_TOKEN=
# COOP_URL=
# COOP_API_KEY=

# Agent mode (README "Agent mode (demo)"): start the stack with --profile agent. Its gateway forwards to
# the model endpoint above, block A, B or C alike.
# The model agent tasks run on. It must be a model your endpoint serves: compose.yaml defaults it to
# LOCAL_MODEL_ALIAS (qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct), which only block A's local llama.cpp serves, so with block B
# or C set it to one of your MODEL_ALLOWLIST ids.
# AGENT_MODEL=mistral-large-2512
# The agent service's URL and token (agentServiceUrl and agentServiceToken in
# web/src/lib/chat/server/agentMode.ts; together they turn agent-mode on, FLAG_KEYS in
# web/src/lib/chat/server/flagKeys.ts). --profile agent sets both for you, so leave them unset; set them
# only to point web at an agent-service of your own, which then replaces the bundled one.
# ALPHA_EDITION=selfhost makes any DEPLOY_ENV eligible (agentModeDeployment in agentMode.ts), and
# agent-mode-access is on in self-host (FLAG_KEYS), so it needs no ALPHA_FLAGS entry.
# With ALPHA_EDITION=selfhost, DEPLOY_ENV=production runs with no safety classifier; turns go unscreened.
# AGENT_SERVICE_URL=
# AGENT_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# Connect Google (README "Connect Google"): start the stack with --profile google and set your own Google
# OAuth client's two values. A signed-in person can then connect their Gmail, Calendar and Drive, read-only, in
# Settings → Connectors. The connector's address defaults to the bundled one; set it only to use your own.
# GOOGLE_CONNECT_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CONNECT_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_CONNECTOR_URL=

# Local model endpoints people add in Settings (local-inference, on in self-host) may point only at public
# addresses unless you allow private ones (privateLocalAddressesAllowed in
# web/src/lib/chat/server/localEndpointFetch.ts). "allow" lets them reach localhost, your LAN and your VPC:
# set it only when everyone who can sign in may reach this host's network, as on a single-user install.
# Cloud-metadata addresses (169.254.0.0/16, fd00:ec2::254) are refused either way.
# LOCAL_INFERENCE_INTERNAL_ADDRESSES=allow

# People's files are stored in PLAINTEXT unless sealing is on (sealWritesOn in
# web/src/lib/chat/server/storage/custodialStorage.ts), and sealing needs a master key (masterSet in
# web/src/lib/chat/server/storage/custodialCrypto.ts). Make an identity with `age-keygen -pq`, then:
# CUSTODIAL_SEAL_WRITES=true
# CUSTODIAL_MASTER_IDENTITIES={"k1":"AGE-SECRET-KEY-PQ-1..."}
# CUSTODIAL_MASTER_ACTIVE=k1

# Mail for sign-in codes and workspace invitations (README "Sign-in"). With none, people sign in with an
# email address and a password instead, and nobody checks the address is theirs.
# SMTP_URL=smtp://user:password@smtp.example.org:587
# SMTP_FROM=Alpha <noreply@example.org>
# With no mail, the first account made on the stack is yours and sign-up closes after it (README
# "Sign-in"). Set this to let anyone who reaches the stack create an account.
# ALPHA_OPEN_SIGNUP=true

# Web search and read-aloud hosts. Each defaults to the hosted service, so leave both unset unless you
# run your own.
# Firecrawl is AGPL and self-hostable with the same v2 API; search and scrape go to <base>/v2/...
# FIRECRAWL_BASE_URL=http://firecrawl:3002
# With no FIRECRAWL_API_KEY, --profile searxng runs SearXNG for open-web search (README.md, "Open-web
# search without a Firecrawl key"). Firecrawl, when it has a key, keeps the slot.
# SEARXNG_URL=http://searxng:8080
# Optional: SearXNG's own cookie secret. Unset, each start picks a random one, which the app never needs.
# SEARXNG_SECRET=
# Search cites a page only once a reranker has scored it as relevant, so with no reranker every searched
# turn answers without sources. With --profile rerank, the bundled bge-reranker-v2-m3 on CPU (README.md,
# "Web search with sources"); RERANK_SCORES=logits because llama.cpp returns raw scores:
# RERANK_MODEL=BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3
# RERANK_ENDPOINT_URL=http://rerank:8080
# RERANK_SCORES=logits
# Read-aloud takes any OpenAI-compatible /audio/speech endpoint; Alpha posts to <base>/audio/speech.
# READ_ALOUD_BASE_URL=http://speech:8880/v1
# Bearer key for that endpoint; unset sends none, and no Hugging Face token is needed.
# READ_ALOUD_API_KEY=
# Model and voice names that endpoint expects; unset sends the Kokoro model and the Kokoro voice for the clip's language.
# READ_ALOUD_MODEL=
# READ_ALOUD_VOICE=

# Everything else compose reads, each with a working default in compose.yaml (or the file named). Set one
# only to change it; selfhost/README.md's settings tables say more.
# Local model (--profile local): which GGUF llama.cpp downloads, the name it serves, and its context size.
# LOCAL_MODEL_REPO=
# LOCAL_MODEL_FILE=
# LOCAL_MODEL_ALIAS=
# LOCAL_MODEL_CTX=
# Safety screen (--profile safety): the Shieldstral GGUF and where it downloads from.
# SAFETY_MODEL_FILE=
# SAFETY_MODEL_URL=
# Reranker (--profile rerank): the bge-reranker-v2-m3 GGUF and where it downloads from.
# RERANK_MODEL_FILE=
# RERANK_MODEL_URL=
# How many documents a search gives the reranker, how much of each it scores, and how long it waits (ms).
# Compose's 12, 600 and 8000 assume 4 cores; on 2 cores set 6 and 300 (README "Web search with sources").
# RERANK_MAX_DOCS=
# RERANK_DOC_CHARS=
# RERANK_TIMEOUT_MS=
# How long document RAG waits for the reranker to score its top five passages (ms). Compose's 6000 covers
# 2 cores (5.8 s at p95); only RAG_ENABLED with an embedder reaches it.
# RAG_RERANK_TIMEOUT_MS=
# Which passages document RAG keeps: at least RAG_RERANK_MIN_SCORE, and at least RAG_RERANK_RELATIVE_RATIO
# of the best passage's score. Compose's 0.005 and 0.05 are tuned for the bundled bge-reranker-v2-m3 in
# French, Spanish and German; tune both together for another reranker (0 turns the relative cut off).
# RAG_RERANK_MIN_SCORE=
# RAG_RERANK_RELATIVE_RATIO=
# Your own model list in place of the model settings above (README "Your own model list").
# ALPHA_MODELS_FILE=/config/models.json
# How long a turn may run, and may go without a new token, in ms.
# GENERATION_TIMEOUT_MS=
# SILENCE_TIMEOUT_MS=
# Cookies over plain http (true by default, for localhost); set false once you serve https.
# ALLOW_INSECURE_COOKIES=
# The description in the page's metadata.
# PUBLIC_APP_DESCRIPTION=
# Only for a Vercel-style cron calling /chat/api/cleanup; the server runs its daily cleanup without it.
# CRON_SECRET=
# My files in a Hugging Face bucket of your own, beside your store (the storage-locations flag).
# HF_BUCKETS_TOKEN=
# HF_BUCKETS_REPO=
# Agent mode: the engine in the sandbox (kilo, or hermes), and the Docker socket's group on the host
# (`stat -c %g /var/run/docker.sock`).
# AGENT_ENGINE=
# DOCKER_SOCK_GID=
# GPU override (compose.gpu.yaml): how many layers llama.cpp puts on the GPU.
# LLAMA_ARG_N_GPU_LAYERS=
# Images, each pinned by digest in the compose file that uses it; change one only to a digest you trust.
# ALPHA_IMAGE (compose.release.yaml) picks another release tag of the app image.
# ALPHA_IMAGE=
# LLAMACPP_IMAGE=
# POSTGRES_IMAGE=
# SEAWEEDFS_IMAGE=
# SOCKGUARD_IMAGE=
# GOOGLE_CONNECTOR_IMAGE=
